.SH DESCRIPTION
These commands hold a conversation about code with an AI model through an OpenAI
compatible API, either OpenAI itself or a local server such as Ollama, LM Studio
or llama.cpp. The chat completions API is used by default, or OpenAI's
responses API when \fB%ai-api\fR is \fCresponses\fR, which some newer models
need.
.PP
The conversation is the text of the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer. Each part starts
with a line holding just \fC## You\fR, \fC## AI\fR or \fC## System\fR, which may
end in an HTML comment, anything before the first of these is ignored. A reply's
line notes the model, the effort and the tokens it used, for example
.CS
## AI <!-- gpt-5.1, high effort, 15302 tokens in (14080 cached), 912 out (640 reasoning) -->
.CE
A new chat starts with a comment listing its keys, which
.B ai-chat-keys
puts back at the top of any chat. A role line inside a fenced code block, one
fenced by three or more backticks or tildes and ended by a line of at least as
many of the same, is part of the block's text. A code block that a question or a
reply leaves open is closed before the next part is added, and
.B ai-send
will not send a chat in which an open block hides the parts after it. The whole
chat is sent with every question, so earlier questions and answers may be edited
or deleted to change what the model sees, and the chat may be carried on later
from any buffer holding these lines. A \fC## System\fR part replaces the system
prompt given by \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR. The buffer uses the markdown major mode
with a hilighting of its own, worked out from the top of the buffer so the
colours do not change as the chat is scrolled. Only a fenced code block carries
on from one line to the next, its fences found as above, while emphasis, links,
strings and comments end with their line, so code pasted outside a block cannot
spoil the colours of the rest of the chat. Code blocks are drawn in one colour,
as a language's own hilighting could leave a string open past the block's end.
The role lines stand out from the headings, each role on its own colour of
background, \fC##\ You\fR on yellow, \fC##\ AI\fR on cyan, \fC##\ System\fR on
magenta and \fC##\ Tool\fR on green, from the colour scheme's highlight colours, with the
comment after one, such as the model that wrote a reply, drawn as a comment.
.PP
Some of the chat is the editor's and is not sent back as the model's words: a
note such as \fC[AI: cancelled]\fR or \fC[AI error: ...]\fR, a reply's thinking
block, and the status lines written after edit blocks, which are instead
reported at the start of the next question.
.PP
Files are given to the model by attaching them to a question. An attachment is
a single line in a \fC## You\fR part, so the chat stays short:
.CS
@file ~/src/exec.c
@file ~/src/exec.c:2133-2193
@buffer *scratch*
@cmd make 2>&1 | tail -40
@diff ~/src
@tree ~/src
.CE
The first attaches a whole file, the second only lines 2133 to 2193 and the
third a buffer with no file. The text is read when the question is sent, from
the file's buffer when it is loaded, so unsaved changes are included, otherwise
from the disk, and every later question sends it again as it is then. When the
same attachment appears in more than one question its text is only sent with
the last, the earlier ones pointing to it. The model is told each file's name
as the shortest end of its path that tells it apart from the other attached
files, e.g. \fCsrc/exec.c\fR. A file that cannot be read, such as a directory
or a binary file, or a range past its end, is noted in the request and in the
message line, and a range that runs past the end is cut short. Deleting the
line removes the attachment, and one inside a fenced code block is just text.
.PP
The other three run a command when the question is sent, in the editor's current
directory, and send what it writes, up to \fB%ai-attach-cmd-lines\fR lines:
\fC@cmd\fR runs the given shell command, asking first the first time it is run
in each chat; \fC@diff\fR sends \fCgit diff HEAD\fR of the file or directory, so
the changes not yet committed; and \fC@tree\fR lists the files of the directory,
from \fCgit ls-files\fR or, outside git, by reading the directories, leaving out
those starting with a dot and links to directories. A file an edit names that is not attached is then
taken to be relative to the tree's directory. These need \fCgit\fR, and a shell
for \fC@cmd\fR.
.PP
.B ai-chat
shows the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer in a popup window and moves to its end, ready
for a question to be written under the last \fC## You\fR line. Given an
argument it asks for the name of a chat, with completion, and shows that chat,
a buffer named \fC*ai-chat:\fIname\fC*\fR, starting it when there is none; an
empty name is \fC*ai-chat*\fR. The chat last shown or sent from is the one the
commands below use from other buffers.
.PP
.B ai-send
sends the chat in the current buffer, the last part of which must be a
\fC## You\fR question. The reply is added under a new \fC## AI\fR line, followed
by an empty \fC## You\fR for the next question. Each chat may have one request
running, so several chats can wait for their replies at once, each from its own
server and model; a command that would change a chat whose request is running says
it is busy. While a request runs its chat's mode line says how it is going, such as
\fC[AI thinking 12s]\fR or \fC[AI writing 30s]\fR, and when it ends the message
line gives the tokens it used and the chat's total, naming the chat when there is
more than one. The questions about a reply that is in, such as whether its tool
calls may run or its edits be kept, are asked one chat at a time, and not while
another command is asking something or building a request. A reply cut short by the
model's output limit or by a content filter is noted after it. A request that
gets no connection, or a rate limit or server error, before any of the reply
has arrived is sent again after \fB%ai-retry-wait\fR seconds, twice as long each
time, or as long as the server asks with a \fCRetry-After\fR header when the
reply streams through \fCcurl\fR, up to \fB%ai-retries\fR times;
\fCC-c C-k\fR stops the wait. It is not sent again when the account is out of
credit or the server asks for a wait of over two minutes. When the key comes
from \fB%ai-api-key-command\fR and the server refuses it, as when it has run
out, the command is run again for a new key and the request sent again, once.
.PP
When the chat ends in a reply whose tool calls have no results, as when their
review was missed or the chat was saved before it,
.B ai-send
reviews them, see below, and sends the chat with their results. A tool call that
is left with no result, such as one a question was written after, is not sent,
as the API would refuse it.
.PP
.B ai-preview
shows what
.B ai-send
would send for the chat, without sending it, in the \fC*ai-request*\fR buffer:
the model, effort, profile and server, the size in characters with a guess of
the tokens, and each message as text under a heading such as \fC## system\fR or
\fC## user\fR, with the attachments read as they are now, the system prompt
and its edit block instructions, and what happened to the last reply's edits.
The JSON sent is a little larger, as its text is escaped. A \fC@cmd\fR
attachment is run to show what it writes.
.PP
In a chat buffer the following keys are bound, and listed at the top of a new
chat:
.IP "\fC\*cc \*cc\fR"
.B ai-send
.IP "\fC\*cc \*ck\fR"
.B ai-cancel
.IP "\fC\*cc g\fR"
.B ai-retry
.IP "\fC\*cc >\fR"
.B ai-continue
.IP "\fC\*cc l\fR"
.B ai-preview
.IP "\fC\*cc f\fR"
.B ai-attach
.IP "\fC\*cc v\fR"
.B ai-show-attachment
.IP "\fC\*cc q\fR"
.B ai-quote
.IP "\fC\*cc y\fR"
.B ai-paste
.IP "\fC\*cc e\fR"
.B ai-edit
.IP "\fC\*cc a\fR"
.B ai-apply-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc d\fR"
.B ai-review-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc u\fR"
.B ai-undo-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc s\fR"
.B ai-save-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc j\fR"
.B ai-redo-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc i\fR"
.B ai-insert-block
.IP "\fC\*cc m\fR"
.B ai-set-model
.IP "\fC\*cc r\fR"
.B ai-set-effort
.IP "\fC\*cc p\fR"
.B ai-set-profile
.IP "\fC\*cc t\fR"
.B ai-system
.IP "\fC\*cc n\fR"
.B ai-new
.IP "\fC\*cc o\fR"
.B ai-chat-open
.IP "\fC\*cc b\fR"
.B ai-fork
.IP "\fC\*cc \*cn\fR, \fC\*cc \*cp\fR"
.BR ai-next-part ,
.B ai-prev-part
.IP "\fC\*cc \*cf\fR, \fC\*cc \*cb\fR"
.BR ai-next-block ,
.B ai-prev-block
.IP "\fC\*cc tab\fR"
.B ai-fold
.IP "\fC\*cc ?\fR"
.B ai-chat-keys
.LP
.B ai-ask
prompts for a one line question, adds it to the chat and sends it, leaving the
cursor where it was while the reply appears in the chat window.
.PP
.B ai-code-action
sends code from the current buffer with an instruction. The code is the lines of
the active region, or the whole buffer when there is none, and is attached to
the question rather than copied into the chat, together with anything already
attached to a question that has not been written yet. The user is prompted for a question, or one of the
actions listed in \fB%ai-actions\fR, which are offered as the prompt's history with the up and down keys. The
actions are listed below. For an action that asks for the complete code, the
model is asked for edit blocks instead when the whole buffer is sent. The
actions are:
.IP \fBexplain\fR
Explain what the code does.
.IP \fBreview\fR
List bugs, edge cases and readability problems, most serious first.
.IP \fBfix\fR
Reply with the corrected code and a list of the changes.
.IP \fBdocument\fR
Reply with the code with documentation comments added.
.IP \fBtests\fR
Write unit tests for the code.
.IP \fBsimplify\fR
Reply with simpler code that behaves the same.
.LP
The text of action \fIname\fR is held in \fB%ai-action-\fIname\fR, so an action
may be changed, or a new one added to \fB%ai-actions\fR with its own variable.
.PP
.B ai-insert-block
copies a fenced code block from the chat. In the chat it uses the block holding
the cursor, or the last block before it; from any other buffer it uses the last
block of the chat. When the block holds changes, as an edit block or another
form described below, they are applied as by
.BR ai-apply-edits .
Otherwise the user is asked whether to replace the region
last sent by
.B ai-code-action
with it (\fCr\fR, only offered when a region was sent), to insert it at the
cursor of that buffer (\fCi\fR) or just to copy it to the kill buffer and the
system clipboard (\fCc\fR). The choice may instead be given as an argument. A
replacement can be repeated with a later block, as the region is moved to cover
the new code.
.PP
.B ai-attach
adds an attachment to the question being written, starting a new \fC## You\fR
part when the chat does not end in one. In the chat it prompts for files, with
completion, one after another until \fCRETURN\fR is pressed on the directory
that the prompt starts with, or given an argument for buffers, such as
\fC*compile*\fR, until an empty name; a buffer of a file is attached as the file.
In any other buffer it attaches without the line numbers having to be typed,
and without the windows changing:
.BS
with an active region, the lines of the region,
.BU
on a line of a \fC*grep*\fR or compile buffer, the lines around that match,
.BU
otherwise it asks whether to attach the function around the cursor, found with
the patterns the major mode uses to collapse functions and taking in the
comment above it, or the whole file. Where the mode has none, or the cursor is
not in a function, the lines around the cursor are attached.
.BU
in a diff, such as \fC*svn-diff*\fR, it asks instead whether to attach the
changes to the file at the cursor, from its \fCIndex:\fR or \fCdiff\fR line, or
the whole diff.
.BE
A buffer that holds the output of a command, such as \fC*svn-diff*\fR or
\fC*grep*\fR, has its directory as its file name, so it is attached as a
buffer; this also goes for
.B ai-code-action
and
.BR ai-edit .
An attachment of a file already attached to the question is merged with any
ranges it overlaps or touches, or replaces them when it is the whole file.
Attaching again is also how a range is moved after the file has been edited.
.PP
.B ai-show-attachment
shows the file or buffer of the attachment line at the cursor in another
window, with the attached lines selected, or runs a \fC@cmd\fR, \fC@diff\fR or
\fC@tree\fR line and shows what it writes.
.PP
When a chat has attachments of files or buffers the model is asked, by
\fB%ai-edit-prompt\fR, to change a file with edit blocks rather than send all of
it back. An edit block is a fenced block whose info string is \fCedit\fR and the
file's name as given in its attachment, holding one or more changes:
.CS
```edit src/exec.c
<<<<<<< SEARCH
        return mlwrite(MWABORT|MWCLEXEC,(meUByte *)"[Failed to load file %s]", fname);
=======
        return mlwrite(MWABORT|MWCLEXEC,(meUByte *)"[Cannot find %s]", fname);
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```
.CE
As models do not always keep to this, with \fB%ai-edit-lenient\fR set (the
default) these are taken as changes too: a block fenced with tildes, a name
given as \fCedit: \fIname\fR or \fCedit path=\fIname\fR, a code block of
\fCSEARCH\fR and \fCREPLACE\fR changes whose file is named on the line above it,
such as \fC**src/exec.c**\fR, and a unified diff, each hunk of which is a change
whose \fCSEARCH\fR is its context and removed lines. A diff may create a file
but not remove one.
.PP
.B ai-apply-edits
applies the changes of the reply at or before the cursor, or of the last reply
of the chat when run from another buffer. The lines of each \fCSEARCH\fR
are looked for in the file, exactly, then ignoring white space at the ends of
lines, then ignoring indentation, in which case the replacement takes on the
file's indentation: tabs for spaces or spaces for tabs a level at a time when
the model used the other, else moved by as many columns as the model's lines
were out. The \fCSEARCH\fR text, even one of blank lines only, must be found
once, or once within the attached lines of the file. A file is named as in its
attachment, by the end of its path, which must fit only one attached file, or by
a path from where the attachments' names start; quotes, a leading \fC./\fR and
line numbers after the name are ignored. A block the model left open is closed
before the next block or the end of the reply, and an edit block shown inside
another code block is not applied. Each change is made and shown selected in
another window, and the user is asked whether to keep it: \fCy\fR keeps it,
\fCn\fR puts the old lines back, \fCa\fR keeps it and the rest without
asking and \fCq\fR, or \fCC-g\fR, puts it back and stops. With an argument of
\fC0\fR every change is kept without asking.
.PP
.B ai-review-edits
shows all the pending changes of the reply at once, as a diff in the
\fC*ai-diff*\fR buffer, each headed by its file and line and by whether it would
fail, create a file or change \fC%\fR formats. Nothing is changed until it is
applied. In it \fCy\fR keeps the change at the cursor and \fCn\fR rejects it,
\fCu\fR undecides it, \fCa\fR keeps and \fCr\fR rejects all those not yet
decided, \fCtab\fR moves to the next change, \fCC-c C-c\fR applies the changes
kept and marks those rejected, leaving the rest to be applied later, and \fCq\fR
quits. The changes must still be those reviewed, otherwise it asks for the reply
to be reviewed again.
.PP
The question warns when the printf style \fC%\fR formats of the lines look
changed, as a reworded message may then no longer match the arguments of its
call, and such a change is asked about even when the changes are being kept
without asking, unless it was kept in the review. The check is a guide only: it
compares the arguments the formats take, their types, sizes and \fC*\fR widths,
ignoring flags, widths and precisions, and knows nothing of other kinds of
format.
.PP
Changing a file that is not attached is only done once the user agrees, and an
empty \fCSEARCH\fR creates the file, if it does not exist, in a new buffer, or
fills an empty file or buffer. The user is asked once for each such file in a
run, even when the changes are being kept without asking, which only lets an
attached file be created without asking. A buffer in view mode is not changed.
Nothing is saved. As each change is made, kept or not, a line is added after
its block giving what happened to it, such as
.CS
(edit 1 applied, exec.c line 2163)
.CE
so it is not applied again; deleting the line lets that change be applied again.
The model is told what happened to its changes at the start of the next
question rather than seeing these lines in its reply, and lines like them that
the model writes in a reply are removed. When a kept change adds or removes
lines the line ranges of the file's attachments are moved to match.
.PP
The files that kept changes are left unsaved, as the message says, unless
\fB%ai-edit-save\fR is set.
.B ai-save-edits
saves the files changed by the edits kept, and undone, that have not been saved
since, from any buffer.
.PP
.B ai-redo-edits
asks the model again for the edits of its last reply that failed. It adds
\fB%ai-redo-prompt\fR to the question being written, after any words already
there, and sends it; the request starts by telling the model what happened to
each edit and why, and the attached files are sent as they are now. The edits
of the reply are reviewed once it is in, as for
.BR ai-edit .
.PP
.B ai-undo-edits
puts back the lines changed by the last
.B ai-apply-edits
that kept a change, and marks each change it undoes as \fCundone\fR. A change
whose lines have been moved by lines added or removed above them is found again,
but one whose lines have been edited since is left. A change that only removed
lines is put back between the lines that were either side of it, so it is left
when those lines, up to three each side, have been edited or no longer fit one
place. A
file created by an edit has its buffer deleted, unless it has been changed or
saved since. A change in a buffer now in view mode is left.
.PP
.B ai-edit
asks for one change to code, such as rewording an error message. Run from a
file, it attaches the file, or the lines of the region when there is one; run
from the chat it uses the files already attached. It then prompts for:
.BS
the text to quote, which starts as the first line of the text last copied, from
the system clipboard or the kill buffer, so a message copied from the code only
needs \fCRETURN\fR. Clearing it leaves the question without a quote.
.BU
how it should change. This is either typed, or the name of a template from
\fB%ai-edit-actions\fR, which are offered as the prompt's history with the up and down keys. A template whose text holds \fC%s\fR
then prompts for the words to put there.
.BE
The question is added to the chat and sent, and once the reply is in, when it
holds edit blocks, they are reviewed as by
.BR ai-apply-edits ,
starting once no key has been pressed for a second so that keys being typed
elsewhere are not taken as answers.
The template \fBmessage\fR rewrites the quoted message as described, keeping
its \fC%\fR formats matching the arguments. For example, after copying a line
that prints \fC[Failed to load file %s]\fR, \fCai-edit\fR, \fCRETURN\fR,
\fCmessage\fR and \fCdrop the word file\fR sends:
.CS
## You
@file ~/microemacs/src/exec.c

```text
return mlwrite(MWABORT|MWCLEXEC,(meUByte *)"[Failed to load file %s]", fname);
```
Rewrite the message quoted above, which comes from the attached code, as
follows: drop the word file. ...
.CE 0
.PP
.B ai-fix-error
is run on the line of an error in a \fC*grep*\fR or compile buffer. It sends the
message, that line and the indented lines after it, with the function of the
file it points to, or the lines around it, attached, asking with
\fB%ai-fix-prompt\fR for a fix, whose edits are reviewed once the reply is in as
for
.BR ai-edit .
.PP
.B ai-commit-message
writes a commit message for the changes staged in git, into the current buffer
at the cursor, such as the message buffer git opens when \*(mS is its editor.
It attaches the staged diff of the work tree of the buffer's file as a
\fC@cmd\fR line, asks with \fB%ai-commit-prompt\fR, and inserts the code block
of the reply once it is in.
.PP
.B ai-retry
asks the last question again, replacing its reply and anything after it, such as
after an error or with another model or effort. It asks first when the reply's
edits were applied, and will not lose a question that has been written but not
sent. Given an argument it prompts for a model and effort to ask with this time
only, so models can be compared without changing the chat's.
.PP
.B ai-continue
has the model go on with its last reply, such as one cut short by the model's
output limit or a lost connection. The editor's notes and the empty question
after the reply are removed, the model is asked to continue from exactly where
it stopped with \fB%ai-continue-prompt\fR, which is not added to the chat, and
what it writes is added to the end of the reply, inside the code block it was
writing when it was cut off in one.
.PP
.B ai-quote
adds the text of the region, in the chat or any other buffer, to the question
being written, each line starting with \fC>\fR. The windows stay as they are:
from another buffer the chat is changed in its window when it is shown, else
out of sight.
.PP
.B ai-paste
pastes the text last copied, as \fByank\fR would, into the question being
written as a fenced code block, at the cursor when it is in the question and
otherwise at its end. The block is tagged with the major mode of the buffer in
the window last used before the chat's, which is usually where the text was
copied, and is left untagged for plain text; given an argument it asks for the
language. The fence has more backticks than any line of the text.
.PP
.B ai-fork
starts a new chat, \fC*ai-chat:\fIn\fC*\fR, holding the chat up to the end of
the part at the cursor, so the conversation can go on from there another way,
or a question be asked again differently.
.PP
.BR ai-next-part " and " ai-prev-part
move to the next or previous role line,
.BR ai-next-block " and " ai-prev-block
to the next or previous code block, and
.B ai-fold
folds the part at the cursor so only its role line shows, or opens a part that
is folded. The chat is unfolded when it is sent or changed by these commands.
.PP
Chats are saved as they are sent and replied to when \fB%ai-chat-save\fR is 1,
the default, each in a file of \fB%ai-chat-dir\fR named by when it was started
and its first question, such as \fC2026-11-12-1530-why-does-the-parser-fail.md\fR.
.B ai-chat-open
prompts for one of them, with completion and the newest offered first, and opens
it in the chat window in place of the chat there, which is saved first; going on
with it carries on saving to its file. A saved chat that ends in tool calls with
no results is left that way for
.B ai-send
to review them. Given an argument it lists the saved chats instead, newest
first, in the \fC*ai-saved*\fR buffer, where \fCRETURN\fR opens the chat on
the line, \fCd\fR deletes it after asking, \fCr\fR renames it, \fCs\fR
lists only the chats holding some text, ignoring case, with the first line that
does, \fCa\fR lists them all again and \fCq\fR quits. The chat open in a chat
buffer cannot be deleted, and one renamed goes on saving under its new name. The
search needs \fCgrep\fR.
.B ai-new
starts a new chat in the chat window; when chats are not saved it asks first if
the chat holds any replies.
.PP
.B ai-cancel
stops the request of the chat, the current buffer or else the chat last used: a
reply that is streaming in, keeping what has arrived so far, or the wait before a
request is sent again. Requests in other chats go on.
.PP
.B ai-set-model
fetches the list of models from the server and prompts for one, with
completion. The models whose names match \fB%ai-model-skip\fR, which are not for
chat, are left out, and the list is kept for the session unless an argument is
given. If the list cannot be fetched any name may be typed. The model is
saved in the registry, so it is remembered by later sessions; when no model has
been set the first request prompts for one.
.PP
.B ai-set-effort
sets how hard a reasoning model thinks before it replies, sent as the
\fCreasoning_effort\fR of the request, or the \fCreasoning\fR effort of the
responses API. It prompts with completion from
\fB%ai-efforts\fR, though any value the server takes may be typed; more effort
gives a more careful reply that is slower and costs more. An empty effort, the
default, is not sent, leaving it to the model, which a model that does not
reason needs as it may refuse the request otherwise. The effort is saved in the
registry like the model and is shown in the message line while a request is
sent.
.PP
Each chat has its own profile, model and effort, which are used whenever it is
sent and shown in its mode line, for example \fC[AI gpt-5.1 high, work]\fR, or
the request's model and how it is going while one is sent.
.BR ai-set-model ,
.B ai-set-effort
and
.B ai-set-profile
set those of the chat they are run in, or of the chat last used when run from
another buffer. A new chat starts with those in use, a branch with those of the
chat it came from, and a saved chat that is opened takes those of its last reply,
which its role line records.
.PP
.B ai-system
moves to the chat's \fC## System\fR part, adding one at its top filled with
\fB%ai-system-prompt\fR when it has none, so the instructions the model is given
can be changed for the chat; the part is sent in place of \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR.
.PP
.B ai-set-profile
switches between servers. A profile \fIname\fR listed in \fB%ai-profiles\fR is
given by the variables \fB%ai-profile-\fIname\fB-\fIsetting\fR for any of the
settings \fCbase-url\fR, \fCapi-key\fR, \fCapi-key-command\fR, \fCtransport\fR,
\fCcurl-args\fR, \fCtimeout\fR, \fCapi\fR, \fCextra-json\fR, \fCtools\fR,
\fCsystem-prompt\fR, \fCmodel\fR and \fCeffort\fR; a setting
a profile does not give is that of the \fCdefault\fR profile, the plain
variables. The model and effort last chosen with each profile, and the profile
itself, are kept in the registry.
.PP
With \fB%ai-tools\fR set to 1 the model may ask to read lines of a file of the
project, search the project's files with \fCgrep\fR, or list them. The project
is the directory of an attached \fC@tree\fR, else the directory the
attachments' names start from. Each request is shown and only run when the user
allows it: \fCy\fR runs it, \fCn\fR refuses it, \fCa\fR runs it and the rest of
the reply's requests in the project, and \fCq\fR refuses the rest and stops. A
request for a file outside the project is always asked about. The calls are
kept in the reply as \fCtool-call\fR blocks and each result is added as a
\fC## Tool\fR part, then the chat is sent again, up to \fB%ai-tool-rounds\fR
times for a question.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The following variables may be set in the user's \fIuser\fR.emf setup file:
.IP \fB%ai-base-url\fR
The API address, \fChttps://api.openai.com/v1\fR by default.
.IP \fB%ai-api\fR
\fCchat\fR (the default) for the chat completions API, or \fCresponses\fR for
OpenAI's responses API, which is asked not to store the reply. With either the
whole chat is sent each time.
.IP \fB%ai-api-key\fR
The API key. When empty the first line written by \fB%ai-api-key-command\fR is
used, such as \fCpass show openai\fR, run once until it or the profile changes,
else the \fCOPENAI_API_KEY\fR environment variable, either of which avoids
writing the key into a setup file. A local server usually needs no key.
.IP \fB%ai-model\fR
The model, normally set with
.BR ai-set-model .
.IP \fB%ai-model-skip\fR
A regular expression of the model names
.B ai-set-model
leaves out, by default those for embeddings, speech, images, moderation and
the like.
.IP \fB%ai-effort\fR
The reasoning effort, normally set with
.BR ai-set-effort ,
empty to leave it to the model.
.IP \fB%ai-efforts\fR
The efforts
.B ai-set-effort
offers, \fC|none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh|\fR by default. Which of them a
model takes depends on the model and the server.
.IP \fB%ai-extra-json\fR
More members for the request's JSON object, such as
\fC"temperature":0.2,"max_completion_tokens":4000\fR, empty by default.
.IP \fB%ai-profiles\fR
The profiles
.B ai-set-profile
offers, as a list such as \fC|local|work|\fR, see above.
.IP \fB%ai-transport\fR
\fCcurl\fR to stream the reply through \fCcurl\fR, \fChttp\fR to use the built in
HTTP client, or empty (the default) to use \fCcurl\fR when it is found on the
path. \fB%ai-curl\fR gives the \fCcurl\fR program, \fCcurl\fR by default, and
\fB%ai-curl-args\fR more options for it, such as a proxy.
.IP \fB%ai-timeout\fR
The seconds a reply may go without sending anything before it is given up, 600
by default. The built in client's limit is \fB%sock-recv-timeout\fR, which is set
to this when the built in client is first used for a request unless already set,
and only takes effect when no URL has been loaded yet; it is not changed when
\fCcurl\fR is used.
.IP "\fB%ai-retries\fR, \fB%ai-retry-wait\fR"
How many times a request that failed for a reason that may pass is sent again,
2 by default, and the seconds waited before the first time, 5 by default, unless
the server asks for another wait.
.IP \fB%ai-bell\fR
When to ring the bell, or show \fC[BELL]\fR when \fB$quiet\fR is set, as a
reply comes in: \fC0\fR (the default) never, \fC1\fR when the chat is not the
current buffer and \fC2\fR always.
.IP \fB%ai-usage\fR
When 1 (the default) a streamed reply asks for the tokens it used, which a few
servers may not take.
.IP \fB%ai-reasoning\fR
When 1 the thinking that some servers send with a reply, or OpenAI's summary of
it, is shown in a \fCthinking\fR block before the reply, 0 by default. It is
not sent back.
.IP \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR
The instructions sent before the chat. By default they ask for code in fenced
blocks and, for code that is not in an attached file, the complete replacement
when it is to change; \fB%ai-edit-prompt\fR covers attached files.
.IP \fB%ai-request-max\fR
When a request is larger than this many characters, by default
\fB%ai-attach-max\fR or 200000 (about 50000 tokens), the user is asked before it
is sent. The size of a request with attachments, or a large one, is shown in
the message line while it is sent.
.IP \fB%ai-attach-lines\fR
The number of lines either side of a match, or of the cursor when there is no
function, that
.B ai-attach
takes, 30 by default.
.IP \fB%ai-attach-cmd-lines\fR
The most lines of what a \fC@cmd\fR, \fC@diff\fR or \fC@tree\fR attachment
writes that are sent, 2000 by default.
.IP "\fB%ai-chat-save\fR, \fB%ai-chat-dir\fR"
Whether chats are saved, 1 by default, and the directory they are saved in, an
\fCaichat\fR directory in the user's directory by default.
.IP \fB%ai-edit-actions\fR
The templates offered by
.BR ai-edit ,
\fC|message|\fR by default. The text of template \fIname\fR is held in
\fB%ai-edit-action-\fIname\fR, in which \fC%s\fR is replaced by the user's
words, so a template is added by naming it in the list and setting its
variable, for example:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable %ai-edit-actions "|message|comment|"
set-variable %ai-edit-action-comment "Add a short comment above the quoted line saying %s."
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.IP \fB%ai-edit-review\fR
When 1 (the default) the edits of a reply to
.B ai-edit
are reviewed as soon as it is in, set to 0 to review them with
.B ai-apply-edits
instead.
.IP \fB%ai-edit-save\fR
What happens to the files changed by the edits kept:
\fC0\fR (the default) leaves them unsaved for
.BR ai-save-edits ,
\fC1\fR asks whether to save them each time edits are applied and \fC2\fR
saves them.
.IP \fB%ai-continue-prompt\fR
The request
.B ai-continue
sends to have the model go on with its reply.
.IP \fB%ai-edit-prompt\fR
The instructions on edit blocks added to the system prompt, or to a \fC## System\fR
part, when the chat has attachments of files or buffers.
.IP \fB%ai-edit-lenient\fR
When 1 (the default) the other forms of change described above are applied as
well as edit blocks.
.IP "\fB%ai-fix-prompt\fR, \fB%ai-commit-prompt\fR, \fB%ai-redo-prompt\fR"
The questions asked by
.BR ai-fix-error ,
.B ai-commit-message
and
.BR ai-redo-edits .
.IP "\fB%ai-tools\fR, \fB%ai-tool-rounds\fR"
Whether the model may ask for the tools described above, 0 by default, and how
many times a chat is sent again with their results, 10 by default.
.IP \fB%ai-wrap\fR
When 1 (the default) the long prose lines of a reply are split at the buffer's
fill column once it has arrived, leaving code blocks, tables and headings alone.
Set to 0 to keep the reply as sent.
.LP
For example, to use a coding model served by Ollama on the local machine:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable %ai-base-url "http://localhost:11434/v1"
set-variable %ai-model "qwen2.5-coder"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
or to keep that as a profile beside OpenAI, with the key read from a password
store:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable %ai-api-key-command "pass show openai"
set-variable %ai-profiles "|local|"
set-variable %ai-profile-local-base-url "http://localhost:11434/v1"
set-variable %ai-profile-local-api-key-command ""
set-variable %ai-profile-local-model "qwen2.5-coder"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
The chat's keys are only bound in a chat. These commands also work from other
buffers, so may be given global keys; the ones below follow the chat's where
they can and leave \*(mS's own \fC\*cc g\fR, \fC\*cc r\fR, \fC\*cc s\fR and
\fC\*cc z\fR alone:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
global-bind-key ai-chat           "C-c c"
global-bind-key ai-ask            "C-c k"
global-bind-key ai-code-action    "C-c x"
global-bind-key ai-edit           "C-c e"
global-bind-key ai-attach         "C-c f"
global-bind-key ai-quote          "C-c q"
global-bind-key ai-insert-block   "C-c i"
global-bind-key ai-apply-edits    "C-c a"
global-bind-key ai-review-edits   "C-c d"
global-bind-key ai-undo-edits     "C-c u"
global-bind-key ai-fix-error      "C-c w"
global-bind-key ai-cancel         "C-c C-k"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.SH NOTES
These are macros defined in \fCaichat.emf\fR.
.PP
Nothing is sent unless one of the commands above is run, but everything in the
chat, including the text of every attached file and what the attached commands
write, is then sent to the server. Saved chats hold the chat's text, not that of
its attachments.
.PP
Killing a chat buffer saves the chat, stops a request still writing into it and removes the
buffers the editor made for it, and the rest of them once no chat is left, but not the record
.B ai-undo-edits
uses.
Renaming a chat buffer while a reply is coming in stops the reply, saying so in the chat.
.PP
A command run from another buffer leaves that buffer and its view in its window.
Those that show something, such as the chat for
.BR ai-ask ,
the diff of
.B ai-review-edits
or the changes being applied, use another window for it, splitting the window
when it is the only one.
.PP
A \fC@cmd\fR line runs a shell command whenever its question is sent, so a chat
written by someone else is worth reading first; each command is asked about the
first time it is run in a chat. The tools only read files, and only once the user
allows each request.
.PP
The lines of a collapsed part of a buffer are not in the buffer itself, so the
saved file is sent instead, or nothing when the buffer also has unsaved changes,
and an edit to them fails until the part is opened.
.PP
With \fCcurl\fR the reply streams into the chat as it is written and editing can
carry on meanwhile. A cursor left at the end of the reply follows it down to the
new \fC## You\fR, and text typed there is moved under it, ready to be sent next.
A cursor moved anywhere else stays there with its view while the reply comes in
and when it ends, so earlier parts can be read. The API key is passed in a
header file readable only by the user, which is deleted as soon as the reply
starts. The built in HTTP client waits for the whole reply, during which the
editor does not respond; it keeps the key in memory, removing flag \fCd\fR from
\fB%http-flags\fR for the request so the key is not logged in
\fC*http-console*\fR. HTTPS through the built in client needs a version of
\*(mS built with OpenSSL.
.PP
A reply line cannot grow past the 65000 character line limit, so a longer one is
broken. A request is built as UTF-8, which needs a version of \*(mS with Unicode
support for characters outside ASCII.
